[Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  good morning i'm not only barrack chair  2022 i'm also director at the dodge  telecoms regulatory  organization and we are part of the acm  the authority for consumers and markets  three weeks ago i spoke to our  spokesperson let's call him harry and  harry is the one who tries to think of  the clever alliance you put on linkedin  the ones that you try to tell everything  and sometimes turn out to be quite  meaningless  and harry said to me how come i always  say that we support and we enable and we  promote  when are we when can i finally say that  you are doing something  and i said harry welcome to the  regulator  let me say first i'm very grateful and  honored to be in a room with so many  people who are actually doing the  rollout uh harry couldn't make it today  but i'm sure he's jealous because he  would have loved talking to you so thank  you very much for actually making the  rollout work  second i thought it would be good to  make a bit more concrete in what we do  in barrick  and third is i will conclude on the  happy note that i think  that together we are helping to attain  very ambitious targets that eric already  talked about and that roberto viola also  alert to  so what are we doing in beric  as stated before we are the  organization of the european regulators  and we have a one european framework  which aims to create one european single  digital market  so it's very important to have a  consistent  implementation of that same regulatory  framework but at the same time  of course europe consists of different  economies different markets  different topologies different histories  it is  you talk about rollout in rural sector i  mean the rural sector in the netherlands  is  of course very impressive  but it's hard to to actually compare  that to rolling out in the forest of  sweden rolling out on the islands of  greece or rolling out  on the plains of spain  so we have to make sure that we  implement in an effective and efficient  manner a consistent framework that works  in the diversity of the markets that we  have at  hand and that is what we try to do at  beric  and we do that through first of all  monitoring what is out there  what does our rollout look like  how can we help to pinpoint the wide  spots that we have in the various  markets  to make sure that if governments decide  to provide state aid  they actually target the white areas  and they do not crowd out the private  investment  we don't only monitor we also collect  the practices that we see in the markets  to learn and to formulate best practices  to make sure that we stay  within the boundaries of the regulatory  framework  we stay at um and we all have state of  the art  regulatory implementation  and good practices  and we translate those into guidelines  to make sure that we are transparent  to the markets on the way that we  implement our regulatory work  so we monitor we learn we exchange best  practices we formulate guidelines  to make to enhance our consistency and  we also  provide advice on the different  legislative proposals that are  fabricated or thought of in brussels to  make sure that with all the new  regulation they are all consistent with  one another  and trying to reach that same goal  the very ambitious targets of the  digital and green transition  that the chair of the fda council  already alert to  so what does that mean for fiber rollout  first of all as roberto already told you  the european electronic communication  code which is now being implemented  throughout the various nation states  has some very interesting  innovations that we're now putting into  practice as they are being transposed  international legislation  first of all the code tasks us more  clearly than ever before  to not only make sure  that the  the markets are working and that  competition is at a sufficient level it  also tasks us with promoting investments  and the first way to do that is of  course is to make sure that there is an  adequate level of competition because  competition drives investment  the second way is to make sure that we  have a stable regulatory environment  fiber is of course a game for the long  haul  it's not about quick profits  it's about infrastructure that is going  to last us for quite some time so it's  very important to have a stable  regulatory environment to give you the  kind of certainty that you need  and the third very specific example is  that we are we have started a deep  dialogue within barack  on how we make sure that we guide the  transition from copper to fiber in an  orderly  manner  so last year we made an inventory of  what we are all doing and more than half  of the nras in europe  already have policies in place  that exactly address that issue how to  make sure that the copper legacy is  faced off in a very in an orderly and  consistent manner  to make sure that the business case for  fiber is of course going to be a viable  one  the other one is on sharing  and we have always been very favorable  of sharing passive infrastructure be it  ducts be it poles  but as roberto already said we have also  new instruments in the code  so we can offer much more  regulatory recognition  as it were  to proposals to co-invest  and i think italy has already had the  premiere and we're all looking very much  to that example to see how that works  out  but of course france has already had  some experience  that if you have a co-investment plan  and you share your infrastructure you  will be treated that will be taken into  account  when you talk to a regulator about the  regime that is applicable to that  certain network  so sharing given the enormous capacity  that fiverr promises sharing is much  more something that we are very open to  and are really  looking forward to receiving more  proposals as roberto also stated  and then finally regulation  the rollout itself  is of course  not the aim  for us at barack game is connectivity  for  connecting businesses connecting  consumers connecting citizens connecting  people  connecting organizations  so we regulate there where we still see  that we need  to promote the better workings of the  market where that is in order  but what we do see  and i think that is really an important  trend  is that we deregulate more and more in  europe that was of course always the  goal when we started out 20 years ago  start deregulating as soon as possible  we used to regulate actually 18  different markets in telecoms  at present there's only two designated  by the european commission  from 18 to 2. that doesn't make life any  easier by the way because we have also  been tasked with them making sure it is  much more fit for purpose  and that we take into account the real  situation that we have in every single  region  because the rollout of fiber  has shown us very different players  different than what we're used to in  telecom  we have utility companies  that roll out  we have wholesale only companies we have  companies working together rolling out  and they will start in certain parts of  the country they might start in cities  or they might be  specializing in regional  rollout  so that means that more and more nras  look not at their national market but  start looking really at the region  a country like  denmark  i don't think denmark is when you think  of a large country you don't think of  denmark  it's not the first country that comes to  mind  it designated 21 different geographical  markets  and deregulated most of them  on the fiber part  recently  so we are paying closer attention to  what is happening out there  and making more fit for purpose  regulation  as we go about it  so where does that bring us  at berg we've said that the real aim we  have is making sure that we promote  meaningful end-to-end connectivity  serving societal needs  as i said our first strategic objective  is promoting full connectivity  we are technology neutral  but for us full connectivity is in line  with the european digital targets  in very high capacity networks  means a gigabit and serving societal  needs means we take sustainability  as a very important parameter  we support sustainable and open digital  markets we welcome new players we always  look out  whether there's enough competition  enough chances for different companies  to get to run a good business and to  serve customers  and we empower end users  and i think that the way that we have  been  enabling supporting  promoting  helping  has  has brought us in europe with  very ambitious targets and rightfully so  but  we have also come a long way  and i had a look at the numbers for 2020  comparing the european uh homes passed  with uh with the american homes passed  and they're both around 40  and as we already started  in the opening speak to the president of  the council  saying that we already passed a 50 point  and as a regulator i'm not only  interested in homes past i'm interested  in homes connected  homes connected  to high speeds to make sure that  everybody can profit and everybody  can contribute  to europe's digital future  and in that respect thank you so much  for the digital rollout and as i told  harry we're very happy to help  thank you  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you

